## JWT Expiry Check Bug

The most common cause is a **units mismatch**. JWT `exp` is in **seconds**, but `Date.now()` returns **milliseconds**.

### The Bug

```javascript
// BROKEN - comparing milliseconds to seconds
const isExpired = Date.now() > payload.exp;

// Example: Date.now() = 1700000000000 (ms)
//          payload.exp = 1700000000    (seconds)
// 1700000000000 > 1700000000 is ALWAYS true... 
// wait, that would reject everything.

// The opposite mistake is also common:
const isExpired = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000) < payload.exp;
// If you accidentally flip the comparison, expired tokens pass through
```

### Most Likely Culprits

```javascript
// BUG 1: Wrong comparison operator (most common "letting through" cause)
if (Date.now() / 1000 > payload.exp) {  // correct logic
  throw new Error('Token expired');
}
// vs accidentally written as:
if (Date.now() / 1000 < payload.exp) {  // WRONG - never blocks expired tokens
  throw new Error('Token expired');
}

// BUG 2: Units mismatch making expired tokens appear valid
const now = Date.now();          // milliseconds: 1700000000000
const exp = payload.exp;         // seconds:      1700000050
// now > exp is always true, so you might have "fixed" it by flipping
// the operator, which then lets everything through

// BUG 3: Checking the wrong field or missing field entirely
if (payload.expires > Date.now()) { // wrong field name, always undefined = falsy
  throw new Error('Token expired');
}
```

### The Fix

```javascript
function verifyToken(req, res, next) {
  try {
    const token = req.headers.authorization?.split(' ')[1];

    if (!token) {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: 'No token provided' });
    }

    const payload = jwt.decode(token); // or your manual decode

    // ✅ Correct: convert Date.now() to seconds, check exp exists
    const nowInSeconds = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);

    if (!payload.exp) {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Token has no expiry' });
    }

    if (nowInSeconds >= payload.exp) {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Token expired' });
    }

    next();
  } catch (err) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid token' });
  }
}
```

### Better: Use jsonwebtoken's Built-in Verification

```javascript
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');

function verifyToken(req, res, next) {
  try {
    const token = req.headers.authorization?.split(' ')[1];

    if (!token) {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: 'No token provided' });
    }

    // verify() handles expiry, signature, and structure automatically
    const payload = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET);

    req.user = payload;
    next();
  } catch (err) {
    // jwt.verify throws TokenExpiredError, JsonWebTokenError, etc.
    if (err.name === 'TokenExpiredError') {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Token expired' });
    }
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid token' });
  }
}
```

### Quick Debugging Checklist

| Issue | Symptom | Fix |
|-------|---------|-----|
| `<` instead of `>=` | All tokens pass | Flip operator |
| Missing `/1000` | ms vs seconds mismatch | `Date.now() / 1000` |
| Using `jwt.decode()` | No signature/expiry check | Switch to `jwt.verify()` |
| Wrong field name | `undefined` comparison | Check actual payload shape |
| Clock skew | Edge case failures | Add small leeway (30-60s) |

**Share your actual middleware code** if you want a precise diagnosis — the bug is almost certainly one of the above.
